# or mathematically, AQ = QL where A is the original matrix, Q is the eigenvectors, and L is the eigenvalue matrix
# In other words, the product of a matrix with an eigenvector gives a scaled eigenvector.
```

The use of assert is for checking if the computed eigenvectors and eigenvalues are correct. If they are not, then the program will stop execution and an error message will be displayed. The `.allclose()` function checks if two arrays are element-wise equal within a tolerance, which ensures that the values obtained from the two computations are not only the same but also very close.

Note that the eigenvalue `lambda0` corresponds to the first eigenvector `vec` in the original matrix `mtx`. Also, note that the @@@ sign indicates the eigenvector `vec` corresponding to the eigenvalue `lambda0` is a solution to the problem of finding the eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue `lambda0` of the matrix `mtx`.

Lastly, note that the assertion on the eigenvector's property is from linear algebra and states if AQ = QL then Q is a eigenvector of the matrix A (where A is the given original matrix, Q is the eigenvector, and L is the eigenvalue matrix), which demonstrates that the solutions to the eigenvector problem are, indeed, the eigenvectors.

The random generated matrix `mtx` will be different every time the code is run and also the eigenvalue and eigenvector could be different. The values (eigenvalues, eigenvectors) may vary each time because they depend on the random initialization of the matrix `mtx`. So, the results obtained could be different from the results of the previous run or other runs.

Note: There is also alternative python package (e.g. numpy, scipy) or method (e.g. eigh, eigvals) to compute eigenvalues and eigenvectors which could give a better performance especially when working with large scale data. To decide which one to use depends on the context.

One other thing that one might do as well is to normalize the eigenvector which is the principle eigenvector in practice, because the sign of eigenvalues could be changed by multiplying by a complex phase (which doesn't matter if we are only doing mathematical calculations and don't care about phase changes).

Here is a simple way of how to normalize the eigenvector:
```
vec = vec / np.linalg.norm(vec)
``` 

Note, the eigenvectors returned by the eigh function are normalized (unit "length") but the direction of the eigenvector is preserved with the sign of the corresponding eigenvalue being positive. Thus, in many practical applications, one may be more interested in the absolute values of eigenvalues and in the "direction" of eigenvectors. 

If you are using the eigvals function, which computes just the eigenvalues, then you still need to compute the corresponding eigenvectors.

There may be other more sophisticated approaches as well, depending on the size and nature of the data you are working with. And, of course, in some cases one method may be more efficient than another in terms of computational complexity.
